## Header
=pod
.SH "DDC SERVER PROTOCOL"
.IX Header "DDC SERVER PROTOCOL"
This manual page describes the network protocol used by the \s-1DDC\s0
(sub)corpus and distributed servers.  In the syntax descriptions,
the string \*(L"\ex01\*(R" represents a literal \s-1ASCII\s0 byte with decimal value 1
(a.k.a \*(L"\s-1SOH\s0\*(R" a.k.a \*(L"start of heading\*(R"),
and \*(L" \*(R" represents a literal space character (\s-1ASCII\s0 byte with decimal value 32).
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\& ##=====================================================================
\& ## Generic Message Format
\& 
\& LENGTH DATA
\&
\& ##=====================================================================
\& ## Common Messages
\& 
\& version
\& status
\& vstatus
\& info
\& reload DEPTH
\& clear_cache DEPTH
\& 
\& expand_terms "\ex01" PIPELINE "\ex01" TERMS "\ex01" TIMEOUT
\& expand_terms "\ex01" PIPELINE "\ex01" TERMS "\ex01" TIMEOUT "\ex01" CORPUS
\& 
\& get_paradigm " " WORD "\ex01" ILEMMATIZE " " ILANG
\& 
\& ddc_close_socket
\&
\& ##=====================================================================
\& ## Branch\-Server Messages
\& 
\& run_query " " CORPUS "\ex01" QUERY "\ex01" FORMAT "\ex01" OFFSET " " LIMIT " " TIMEOUT
\&
\& ##=====================================================================
\& ## Leaf\-Server Messages
\& 
\& get_first_hits " " QUERY "\ex01 " TIMEOUT " " LIMIT
\& get_hit_strings " " FORMAT "\ex01" OFFSET " " LIMIT
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This section contains descriptions of the various requests which may
be sent to a \s-1DDC\s0 server and their expected responses.
.SS "Generic Message Format"
.IX Subsection "Generic Message Format"
.Vb 1
\& LENGTH DATA
.Ve
.PP
All messages sent to or from a \s-1DDC\s0 server share the common generic format \f(CW\*(C`LENGTH DATA\*(C'\fR,
where \f(CW\*(C`LENGTH\*(C'\fR is a 32\-bit little-endian unsigned integer,
and \f(CW\*(C`DATA\*(C'\fR is the underlying message data, which is exactly \f(CW\*(C`LENGTH\*(C'\fR bytes long.
.SS "Common Messages"
.IX Subsection "Common Messages"
This section describes the messages accepted by both \s-1DDC\s0 \*(L"branch\*(R" and \*(L"leaf\*(R" servers.
.PP
\fIversion\fR
.IX Subsection "version"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& version
.Ve
.PP
Returns the version number of the
running server
as a string, by convention of the form
\&\f(CW\*(C`MAJOR.MINOR.MICRO\-PATCHLEVEL\*(C'\fR
where \f(CW\*(C`MAJOR\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`MINOR\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`MICRO\*(C'\fR are unsigned decimal integers,
and \f(CW\*(C`\-PATCHLEVEL\*(C'\fR is an optional string suffix.
This should be the same string reported by \fBddc_daemon\fR on startup.
.PP
Since v2.0.18
.PP
\fIstatus\fR
.IX Subsection "status"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& status
.Ve
.PP
Returns status information on the running server as a \s-1JSON\s0 object of the form:
.PP
.Vb 10
\& {
\&  "name":"server",                      //\-\- distributed server name
\&  "version":"2.0.18\-pre1",              //\-\- distributed server version
\&  "hostname":"www.dwds.de",             //\-\- distributed server hostname if available (E<gt>= v2.0.35)
\&  "hostaddr":"127.0.0.1",               //\-\- distributed server socket hostname or address ("host" for ddc E<lt> v2.0.35)
\&  "port":50011,                         //\-\- distributed server socket port
\&  "started":"2014\-02\-17T13:49:59Z",     //\-\- ISO\-8601 timestamp of last server start
\&  "uptime":1392644999,                  //\-\- server instance uptime in seconds
\&  "nrequests":21,                       //\-\- number of served requests
\&  "cachesize":42,                       //\-\- size of query cache (leaf servers only)
\&  "curlcachesize":24,                   //\-\- size of curl cache (leaf servers only)
\&  "corpora":["corpus1","corpus2",...]   //\-\- registered subcorpus names (branch servers only)
\& }
.Ve
.PP
Very fast turnaround, since all returned data is known to the distributed server itself,
and no subcorpora need to be queried.
.PP
Since v2.0.18
.PP
\fIvstatus\fR
.IX Subsection "vstatus"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& vstatus
.Ve
.PP
Like the \f(CW\*(C`status\*(C'\fR command, but the elements of the returned \*(L"corpora\*(R" list for a branch server
are the \s-1JSON\s0 objects returned by the \f(CW\*(C`status\*(C'\fR command to the associated subcorpus servers,
rather than just subcorpus names.
.PP
Since v2.0.18.
.PP
\fIinfo\fR
.IX Subsection "info"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& info
.Ve
.PP
Returns information about associated corpora as a \s-1JSON\s0 object.
For a leaf server, the returned object is of the form:
.PP
.Vb 10
\& {
\&  //\-\- basic information
\&  "name":"corpus1",                     //\-\- subcorpus name
\&  "version":"2.0.18\-pre1",              //\-\- subcorpus server version
\&  "project":"corpus1",                  //\-\- project basename
\&  "indexed":"2014\-02\-17T13:49:56Z",     //\-\- index timestamp (mtime of PROJECT._con)
\&  "nfiles":4,                           //\-\- number of successfully indexed files
\&  "nsources":4,                         //\-\- number of source files (including empty and erroneous files)
\&  "nmasked":0,                          //\-\- number of "masked" (blacklisted) files in PROJECT._masked
\&  "ntokens":56294,                      //\-\- number of indexed tokens
\&  //
\&  //\-\- global index options (see ddc_opt(5))
\&  "lang":"German",
\&  "utf8":1,
\&  "caseSensitive":1,
\&  "contextOperator":1,
\&  "dwdsThesaurus":0,
\&  "interpDelimiter":"\eu001f",
\&  "tokenDelimiter":"\eu001e",
\&  "allowUnsafeQueries":0,
\&  "indexPunctuation":1,
\&  "indexMorphPatterns":0,
\&  "indexChunks":0,
\&  //
\&  //\-\- index structure: bibliographic metadata
\&  "bibl":[
\&        {"name":"orig","builtin":1},
\&        {"name":"scan","builtin":1},
\&        {"name":"date","builtin":1},
\&        {"name":"page","builtin":1},
\&        {"name":"author", "visible":1, "size":4},
\&        //... other bibliographic fields ...
\&        {"name":"title", "visible":1, "size":4}
\&        ],
\&  //
\&  //\-\- index structure: break collections
\&  "breaks":[
\&        {"shortname":"s", "longname":"sentence", "size":2823},
\&        //... other break collections ...
\&        {"shortname":"textarea", "longname":"textarea", "size":4}
\&        ],
\&  //
\&  //\-\- index structure: token\-level indices
\&  "indices":[
\&        {"shortname":"w", "longname":"Token", "visible":1, "bigrams":2, "size":9369},
\&        {"shortname":"l", "longname":"Lemma", "visible":1, "size":5706},
\&        //... other token\-level indices ...
\&        {"shortname":"p", "longname":"Pos", "visible":1, "size":53},
\&        ],
\&  //
\&  //\-\- query operators: bibliographic expanders ("ExpandBibl" options)
\&  "xbibl":["xauthor",...]
\&  //
\&  //\-\- query operators: term expanders ("Expand" options)
\&  "expanders":["Token",...],
\&  //
\&  //\-\- user\-supplied information ("ServerInfo" and "ServerInfoFile" options)
\&  "user":{"foo":"bar","baz":["bonk","boffo"]},
\&  }
.Ve
.PP
For a branch server, the returned object has the form:
.PP
.Vb 10
\& {
\&  "name":"server",
\&  "version":"2.0.18\-pre1",
\&  "corpora":[
\&        {
\&         "name":"corpus1",
\&         //... information for subcorpus "corpus1" ...
\&        },
\&        //... information for other subcorpora ...
\&        {
\&         "name":"corpusN",
\&         //... information for subcorpus "corpusN" ...
\&        }
\&  ]
\& }
.Ve
.PP
where each object in the \*(L"corpora\*(R" array is the associated subcorpus server's
response to an \*(L"info\*(R" request.
.PP
Since v2.0.18, user-supplied info since v2.0.34.
.PP
\fIreload\fR
.IX Subsection "reload"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& reload
\& reload DEPTH
.Ve
.PP
Requests re-loading local (sub)corpus data from disk.
If \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\*(C'\fR is present and non-zero, a receiving branch server
will send a \f(CW\*(C`reload\*(C'\fR message with paramter \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\-1\*(C'\fR to any subordinate
servers on the user's behalf; a \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\*(C'\fR parameter of \-1 requests maximum depth.
.PP
Since v2.0.23, \s-1DEPTH\s0 parameter since v2.0.25.
.PP
\fIclear_cache\fR
.IX Subsection "clear_cache"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& clear_cache
\& clear_cache DEPTH
.Ve
.PP
Request that the receiving server immediately clear any in-memory query cache(s).
If \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\*(C'\fR is present and non-zero, a receiving branch server
will send a \f(CW\*(C`reload\*(C'\fR message with paramter \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\-1\*(C'\fR to any subordinate
servers on the user's behalf; a \f(CW\*(C`DEPTH\*(C'\fR parameter of \-1 requests maximum depth.
.PP
Since v2.0.23, \s-1DEPTH\s0 parameter since v2.0.25.
.PP
\fIexpand_terms\fR
.IX Subsection "expand_terms"
.PP
.Vb 2
\& expand_terms " \ex01" PIPELINE "\ex01" TERMS "\ex01" TIMEOUT
\& expand_terms " \ex01" PIPELINE "\ex01" TERMS "\ex01" TIMEOUT "\ex01" CORPUS
.Ve
.PP
Requests online term expansion of the terms \f(CW\*(C`TERMS\*(C'\fR
by the expansion pipeline \f(CW\*(C`PIPELINE\*(C'\fR, which must be known to the leaf server chosen for expansion (see \fIddc_opt\fR\|(5)).
\&\f(CW\*(C`TIMEOUT\*(C'\fR is a request timeout in seconds.
.PP
For branch servers, if \f(CW\*(C`CORPUS\*(C'\fR is specified, it should be the the name of an immediate subcorpus which should
be queried to perform the expansion.  If unspecified, the first registered subcorpus is queried.
.PP
Response data is of the form
.PP
.Vb 1
\& STATUS " " NTERMS "\en" DATA
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR is a decimal integer indicating the operation status (0 indicates success).
\&\f(CW\*(C`NTERMS\*(C'\fR is the number of expanded terms being returned in the \f(CW\*(C`DATA\*(C'\fR portion,
and \f(CW\*(C`DATA\*(C'\fR is either an error message (just in case \s-1STATUS\s0 is non-zero) or a list of expanded terms
separated by TABs, newlines, and/or carriage returns (\s-1ASCII\s0 0x09, 0x0a, 0x0d).
.PP
Since v2.0.0
.PP
\fIget_paradigm\fR
.IX Subsection "get_paradigm"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& get_paradigm " " WORD "\ex01" ILEMMATIZE " " ILANG
.Ve
.PP
Request morphological analysis of the word \f(CW\*(C`WORD\*(C'\fR using legacy built-in language-specific heuristics.
\&\f(CW\*(C`ILEMMATIZE\*(C'\fR is a boolean integer; if true, only lemmatization is performed.
\&\f(CW\*(C`ILANG\*(C'\fR is an integer indicating the built-in language rule-set to use; it should
be a valid MorphLanguageEnum value as defined in ${\s-1DDC_SRC\s0}/src/common/utilit.h ;
currently recognized values are (0:unknown, 1:Russian, 2:English, 3:German, 4:Generic, 5:URL, 6:Digits).
.PP
Response is an \s-1HTML\s0 fragment.
.PP
Since v1.x
.PP
\fIddc_close_socket\fR
.IX Subsection "ddc_close_socket"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ddc_close_socket
.Ve
.PP
Dummy request used internally prior to ddc\-v2.0.23 to avoid
annoying complaints unless each subcorpus socket gets exactly 2 messages per user request.
\&\fB\s-1DANGEROUS\s0\fR to use directly, can cause server lockup!
.PP
Since v2.0.0
.PP
\&\fB\s-1OBSOLETE\s0\fR since v2.0.23.
.SS "Branch-Server Messages"
.IX Subsection "Branch-Server Messages"
This section describes the messages accepted by \s-1DDC\s0 \*(L"branch\*(R" servers,
also known as \*(L"distributed\*(R" or \*(L"nonterminal\*(R" servers.  A branch server is not associated directly
with any single index, but functions as an aggregator for one or more subordinate
(\*(L"leaf\*(R") servers.
.PP
\fIrun_query\fR
.IX Subsection "run_query"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& run_query " " CORPUS "\ex01" QUERY "\ex01" FORMAT "\ex01" OFFSET " " LIMIT " " TIMEOUT
.Ve
.PP
Top-level user query request.
.IP "\s-1CORPUS\s0" 4
.IX Item "CORPUS"
is a vacuous parameter, by convention the string \*(L"Distributed\*(R" is used.
.IP "\s-1QUERY\s0" 4
.IX Item "QUERY"
is the user query in \s-1DDC\s0 syntax (see \fIddc_query\fR\|(5) for details).
.IP "\s-1FORMAT\s0" 4
.IX Item "FORMAT"
is a string representing the data format to be returned by \s-1DDC\s0.
Currently, \s-1DDC\s0 supports the following formats: \*(L"\s-1JSON\s0\*(R", \*(L"\s-1TABLE\s0\*(R", \*(L"\s-1TEXT\s0\*(R", \*(L"\s-1HTML\s0\*(R", or \*(L"\s-1DOCIDS\s0\*(R".
.IP "\s-1OFFSET\s0" 4
.IX Item "OFFSET"
is the index of the first hit to be retrieved, starting from 0 (zero).
.IP "\s-1LIMIT\s0" 4
.IX Item "LIMIT"
is the maximum number of hits to be retrieved.
.IP "\s-1TIMEOUT\s0" 4
.IX Item "TIMEOUT"
is the maximum number of seconds
.PP
Server response depends on \s-1FORMAT\s0.  In \f(CW\*(C`JSON\*(C'\fR mode, the response data
for a traditional context-query is a \s-1JSON\s0 object of the form:
.PP
.Vb 10
\& {
\&   "istatus_" : 0,              //\-\- rsp. istatus_internal status code, 0:success
\&   "nstatus_" : 0,              //\-\- network status code, 0:success
\&   "error_" : null,             //\-\- error string, null:no error
\&   "nhits_" : 31,               //\-\- total number of hits
\&   "dhits_" : "17+4+10=31",     //\-\- distribution of hits over subcorpora
\&   "ndocs_" : 0,                //\-\- (?) number of documents containing hits (?)
\&   "ddocs_" : "0+0+0=0",        //\-\- (?) distribution of hit\-documents over subcorpora (?)
\&   "end_" : 2,                  //\-\- index of last returned hit
\&   "hits_" : [                  //\-\- list of hits
\&     {                                                  //\-\- each hit is an object
\&       "meta_" : {                                      //\-\- hit.meta_: bibliographic metadata
\&            "file_" : "busch_max_1865.xml",             //\-\- indexed filename
\&            "indices_" : ["w","p","l"],                 //\-\- indexed token fields for ctx_[1]
\&            "date_" : "1865",                           //\-\- indexed date (decoded)
\&            "orig_" : "",
\&            "page_" : null,
\&            "scan_" : "",
\&            //... other indexed metadata fields go here ...
\&       },
\&       "ctx_": [                                        //\-\- hit.ctx_ provides result context
\&         [ "Ach",",","was","muß","man","oft",... ],     //\-\- hit.ctx_[0] is left extra\-sentential context (token strings only)
\&         [                                              //\-\- hit.ctx_[1] is the hit sentence itself (structured tokens)
\&           [ 0, "Wie",      "PWAV",    "wie" ],         //\-\- tok[0] is match\-ID, tok[1] is field meta_.indices_[0], etc
\&           ...
\&           [ 1, "Max",      "NE",      "Max" ],         //\-\- tok[0] is nonzero iff token matched a term in the user query
\&           [ 0, "und",      "KON",     "und" ],
\&           [ 2, "Moritz",   "NE",      "Moritz" ],      //\-\- user\-supplied match\-IDs are reported in tok[0]
\&           ...
\&         ],
\&         [ ...,"heimlich","lustig","machen","." ]       //\-\- hit.ctx_[2] is right extra\-sentential context (token strings only)
\&       ],
\&     },
\&     //... other hits go here ...
\&   ]
\& }
.Ve
.PP
As of v2.0.34, ddc supports user-supplied match-IDs via the \f(CW\*(C`=ID\*(C'\fR query operator.
Matched tokens for which an explicit nonzero match-ID was supplied in the query
encode that \s-1ID\s0 as the first element of the returned token-array.
.PP
As of v2.0.23, ddc supports aggregate count-queries by means of the \f(CW\*(C`count()\*(C'\fR operator.
If the requested \f(CW\*(C`QUERY\*(C'\fR was such a count-query, the query response is slightly different:
.IP "nhits_" 4
.IX Item "nhits_"
indicates the best-guess maximum number of non-zero histogram bins for this query.
This number may be smaller than the actual number of non-zero histogram bins
if \f(CW\*(C`LIMIT\*(C'\fR was less than the total number of non-zero bins in some subcorpus
and some non-zero bins occurred in more than one subcorpus:
such duplicate bins are merged by the branch server only, but only up to \f(CW\*(C`LIMIT\*(C'\fR
are retrieved by the branch server for any query, so some uncertainty remains
regarding the status of duplicates among the bins beyond \f(CW\*(C`LIMIT\*(C'\fR.
.IP "dhits_" 4
.IX Item "dhits_"
is of the form \*(L"N1+N2+...=NTOTAL~NMAX\*(R", where the \f(CW\*(C`Ni\*(C'\fR are the number of hits
(non-zero histogram bins) in the associated subcorpora, \f(CW\*(C`NTOTAL\*(C'\fR is the total
number of non-zero histogram bins in any subcorpus (regardless of duplicates),
and \f(CW\*(C`NMAX\*(C'\fR is the best-guess maximum number of non-zero histogram bins as reported
in \f(CW\*(C`nhits_\*(C'\fR.
.IP "hits_" 4
.IX Item "hits_"
is not present.
.IP "counts_" 4
.IX Item "counts_"
is a \s-1JSON\s0 array of the form:
.Sp
.Vb 1
\& [ BIN1, BIN2, ..., BINn ]
.Ve
.Sp
Each element \f(CW\*(C`BINi\*(C'\fR of the \f(CW\*(C`counts_\*(C'\fR array represents a single histogram bin
as a \s-1JSON\s0 array
.Sp
.Vb 1
\& [ COUNT, KEY(s)... ]
.Ve
.Sp
where \f(CW\*(C`COUNT\*(C'\fR is the total count for the bin in question and \f(CW\*(C`KEY(s)...\*(C'\fR
are the key strings as requested in the \f(CW\*(C`QUERY\*(C'\fR \*(L"#BY\*(R"\-clause.
.PP
Since v1.x, \s-1JSON\s0 format and error messages since v2.0.0.
.SS "Leaf Server Messages"
.IX Subsection "Leaf Server Messages"
This section describes the messages accepted by \s-1DDC\s0 \*(L"leaf\*(R" servers,
also known as \*(L"subcorpus\*(R", \*(L"local\*(R", or \*(L"terminal\*(R" servers.
A leaf server is directly associated with exactly one index on the local filesystem,
and has no subcorpora itself.
.PP
\fIget_first_hits\fR
.IX Subsection "get_first_hits"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& get_first_hits " " QUERY "\ex01" TIMEOUT " " LIMIT
.Ve
.PP
Requests subcorpus-local evaluation of the user query \f(CW\*(C`QUERY\*(C'\fR.
\&\f(CW\*(C`TIMEOUT\*(C'\fR is an operation timeout in seconds, and \f(CW\*(C`LIMIT\*(C'\fR is the maximum
number of hits to evaluate.
.PP
If an error occurs, the response is of the form:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& STATUS 0 0 0 0 "\en" ERROR
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR is a non-zero decimal integer error code, and \s-1ERROR\s0 is a text string describing the error.
.PP
On success, responses have the form:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& STATUS " " MAXHIT " " NHITS " " NDOCS " " SORT " " HITIDS
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR is a decimal integer status, 0 (zero) for success,
\&\f(CW\*(C`MAXHIT\*(C'\fR is the index of the last hit computed (?? see CConcHolder::GetHits(q, _\|_EndHitNo_\|_)),
\&\f(CW\*(C`NHITS\*(C'\fR is the number of hits found (?? see CConcHolder::m_AllHitsCount),
\&\f(CW\*(C`NDOCS\*(C'\fR is the number of relevant documents found (?? see CConcHolder::m_RelevantDocumentCount),
\&\f(CW\*(C`SORT\*(C'\fR is a indicates whether and/or how the hits are to be sorted
(0: no sort, 1: ascending sort, \-1: descending sort),
and \f(CW\*(C`HITIDS\*(C'\fR is a TAB-separated list of \f(CW\*(C`NHITS\*(C'\fR hit-identification triples (see \fICConcHolder::GetHitIds()\fR),
each element of which has the form:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& INDEX " " ORDERID " " ORDER_STRING
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`INDEX\*(C'\fR is the query-dependent hit index,
\&\f(CW\*(C`ORDERID\*(C'\fR is an integer, and \f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR is a string.
Prior to ddc version 2.0.19,
\&\f(CW\*(C`ORDERID\*(C'\fR was a global integer primary sort-key for the hit and \f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR
was only non-trivially populated
if the query contained a context-dependend sort operation such as \f(CW\*(C`#left\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`#right\*(C'\fR,
and was otherwise assigned the literal string \*(L"<empty>\*(R".
.PP
As of v2.0.19, \f(CW\*(C`ORDERID\*(C'\fR is the literal string \*(L"\-0\*(R", and
\&\f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR is the (possibly empty) actual sort key for the hit.
If \f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR is the literal string \*(L"<empty>\*(R", it is treated as an empty string for compatibility reasons.
\&\f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR may also be a string of the form \*(L"#XXXXXXXX\*(R" representing a 32\-bit integer
sort key in hexidecimal notation (msb order, high bit set for non-negative numbers only, no fill bits).
.PP
As of v2.0.23, ddc supports aggregate count-queries (by means of the \f(CW\*(C`count()\*(C'\fR operator)
in addition to traditional \*(L"context\*(R" queries.  If the requested \f(CW\*(C`QUERY\*(C'\fR was such a count-query,
then \f(CW\*(C`ORDER_STRING\*(C'\fR is a \*(L"\ex02\*(R"\-separated list of count-key components, and \f(CW\*(C`INDEX\*(C'\fR is the
total count of the associated histogram bin.
.PP
Since v1.x, error message strings since v2.0.0, count-queries since v2.0.23.
.PP
\fIget_hit_strings\fR
.IX Subsection "get_hit_strings"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& get_hit_strings " " FORMAT "\ex01" OFFSET " " LIMIT
.Ve
.PP
Requests verbose hit strings for the current query
(assumedly the query itself was specified in the previous \f(CW\*(C`get_first_hits\*(C'\fR request).
Hits are to be formatted in \f(CW\*(C`FORMAT\*(C'\fR, which should be a format known to \s-1DDC\s0.
\&\f(CW\*(C`OFFSET\*(C'\fR is th offset of the first hit to return, starting from zero,
and \f(CW\*(C`LIMIT\*(C'\fR is the maximum number of formatted hits to return.
.PP
If an error occurred, the response will be of the form:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& STATUS "\ex01" "\en" ERROR
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR is a non-zero decimal integer error code (DDCErrorEnum InternalError),
and \f(CW\*(C`ERROR\*(C'\fR is a text string describing the error.
.PP
On success, the response is of the form:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& STATUS "\ex01" HIT_STRINGS "\ex01"
.Ve
.PP
where \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR is a decimal integer zero (\*(L"0\*(R")
and \f(CW\*(C`HIT_STRINGS\*(C'\fR are the formatted hit strings, separated by \*(L"\ex01\*(R".
By convention, each hit is terminated by \*(L"\en\*(R" and preceded by \*(L"\ex01\*(R".
For \*(L"\s-1JSON\s0\*(R" format hit strings, the first character of the first hit is a \*(L"[\*(R" character,
the final character of all non-final hits is a \*(L",\*(R" character, and the final character
of the final hit is a \*(L"]\*(R" character, so hits can be parsed as raw \s-1JSON\s0 data by removing
the initial \f(CW\*(C`STATUS\*(C'\fR code and all \*(L"\ex01\*(R" characters from \f(CW\*(C`HIT_STRINGS\*(C'\fR before parsing.
.PP
An example response in \s-1JSON\s0 format is:
.PP
.Vb 6
\& 0\ex01[{"meta_":{"file_":"dannhauer_catechismus05_1654.xml",...},"ctx_":[[],[...],[]]},
\& \ex01{"meta_":{"file_":"frentzel_schauplatz_1744.xml",...},"ctx_":[[],[...],[]]},
\& ...
\& \ex01{"meta_":{"file_":"busch_max_1865.xml",...},"ctx_":[[],[...],[]]},
\& \ex01{"meta_":{"file_":"frege_sinn_1892.xml",...},"ctx_":[[],[...],[]]}]
\& \ex01
.Ve
.PP
Since v1.x, \s-1JSON\s0 format and error messages since v2.0.0.
.SS "Error Messages"
.IX Subsection "Error Messages"
This section describes some common error messages which may be returned by a ddc server.
.PP
\fISocket Error! Servname not supported for ai_socktype\fR
.IX Subsection "Socket Error! Servname not supported for ai_socktype"
.PP
This message may be reported by branch server with
a misconfigured \f(CW\*(C`ddc_server.cfg\*(C'\fR file.  In particular, if a subordinated server
is declared in the branch server's \f(CW\*(C`ddc_server.cfg\*(C'\fR with no port number, or with
a colon separating the server address and the port number instead of a space, this
error message will be generated by any attempt to query the server.
